# Facet — Controls

This file defines how a player operates the game: the pointer plays the board
and works every screen, and the keyboard drives the menus from the other side.
The cell centers the pointer is measured against are in `specs/board.md`, and
the rules that decide whether a requested swap is accepted are in
`specs/rules.md`. Every figure below carries the name this specification gives
it.

## The pointer

A mouse, a pen, and a finger on a touchscreen all drive the pointer, and the
game reads them the same way. Everything the pointer does below is done by all
three.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
This game stands on no engine, so the pointer belongs to the runtime layer you
write. What the game reads from that layer is a pointer position in the game's
logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`) stage, a press edge, a
release edge, and which of `mouse`, `pen`, and `touch` drove it. That layer
delivers all three devices on that one path, and it takes the browser's own
gestures on the canvas: panning, pinch-zoom, double-tap zoom, text selection,
and the context menu. A drag on a touchscreen therefore reaches the game whole
rather than being taken for a scroll part way through. How the layer produces
those is yours to decide.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The pointer belongs to the engine, which documents it in the `engine/` directory
at the root of this repository. What the game reads from it is a pointer
position in the game's logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`)
stage, a press edge, a release edge, and the device that drove it.
{{else}}
The pointer belongs to the engine, which documents it in the `engine/` directory
at the root of this repository. What the game reads from it is a pointer
position in the game's logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`)
stage, a press edge, a release edge, and the device that drove it, delivered as
the ordered samples the player controller reads from the engine each frame, so a
drag that crossed several cells between two frames arrives as every position it
visited rather than as the last one alone.
{{/if}}

The game acts on the primary pointer alone, so a second finger resting on the
screen changes nothing. Each pointer position the game reads is resolved on its
own, in the order the positions arrive. `state.pointer` mirrors the position,
the press state, and the device every frame, as `specs/state.md` describes.

## Pointer targets

Every screen carries a set of pointer targets: axis-aligned rectangles the
pointer operates the screen through. A target has an id, a position, and a size
in the stage's logical units, and `specs/instrumentation.md` reports the current
screen's targets under those ids.

| Screen | Targets, in this order |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | `menu-0`, `menu-1`, one per entry of `TITLE_ITEMS` |
| `howto` | `back` |
| `playing` | `pause` |
| `paused` | `menu-0`, `menu-1`, one per entry of `PAUSED_ITEMS` |
| `levelclear` | `menu-0`, `menu-1`, one per entry of `LEVELCLEAR_ITEMS` |
| `gameover` | `menu-0`, `menu-1`, one per entry of `GAMEOVER_ITEMS` |

Every target on every screen satisfies all four of these:

| Requirement | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Width | at least `TARGET_MIN_W` (`96`) |
| Height | at least `TARGET_MIN_H` (`72`) |
| Placement | wholly within the stage |
| Separation | no two targets on one screen overlap |

The width and the height are what a fingertip needs, so every target is worked
by touch as readily as by a mouse.

A `menu-<i>` target covers the drawn menu item that `state.menuIndex` `i`
highlights. The `back` target covers a drawn control reading `BACK_LABEL`
(`BACK`) and the `pause` target one reading `PAUSE_LABEL` (`PAUSE`), each
legible at the stage size. What each target looks like is the build's to design,
within the requirements in `specs/overview.md`.

On `playing` the `pause` target lies wholly outside the board's extent, which
`specs/board.md` gives, so it never covers a cell.

## Operating a screen with the pointer

A pointer position lies in at most one target, since no two on a screen overlap.

| The pointer | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| Moves within a `menu-<i>` target while pressed on nothing | `state.menuIndex` becomes `i`. |
| Presses within a target | The highlight moves as the row above describes, and that target is armed. |
| Releases within the armed target | The target is taken, and it is disarmed. |
| Releases anywhere else | Nothing is taken, and the armed target is disarmed. |

Taking a target does exactly what the same choice does from the keyboard:

| Target | Taken |
| --- | --- |
| `menu-<i>` | The same as `confirm` with `state.menuIndex` at `i`. |
| `back` | The same as the `back` action on that screen. |
| `pause` | The same as the `pause` action. |

A press outside every target arms nothing. Leaving a screen disarms whatever was
armed on it, so a press carried across a screen change takes nothing.

The highlight moving on a press is what makes a touchscreen behave as a mouse
does: a finger has no hover, so the press is what tells the player which item
their release will take.

## Targeting a cell

The board is played on the `playing` screen, everywhere outside that screen's
`pause` target.

The pointer targets the cell whose center is nearest the pointer position, when
that center lies within `GEM_HIT_R` (`36`) of it. `GEM_HIT_R` is half of
`CELL_PITCH` (`72`), so at most one cell center lies strictly within that radius,
and a position lying exactly `GEM_HIT_R` from two centers targets the cell at the
lower row, and within one row the cell at the lower column. A pointer farther than
`GEM_HIT_R` from every cell center targets no cell.

## Selecting, offering, and playing a move

A move is made by taking hold of a gem and letting go of it beside its neighbor.
The gem the player has hold of is the one at `state.selection`, and the neighbor
it is currently being offered into is `state.offer`. Nothing reaches the move
rules until the pointer is released.

A press targets a cell as above. The rows below are evaluated in order, and the
first row that matches is the one that applies.

| Targeted cell | What the press does |
| --- | --- |
| Any cell, while nothing is selected | Selects that cell, with no offer standing. |
| The selected cell | Leaves it selected and withdraws any standing offer, so the gem is taken hold of again where it started. |
| A cell orthogonally adjacent to the selected cell | Offers the selected gem into that cell. |
| Any other cell | Moves the selection to that cell, with no offer standing. |
| No cell, the press lying farther than `GEM_HIT_R` from every cell center | Clears the selection and any offer, leaving the board as it stands. |

While the pointer is held down, each position it reaches is read against the
selected cell:

| The pointer moves within `GEM_HIT_R` of | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| The selected cell | The standing offer is withdrawn, so nothing is offered. |
| A cell orthogonally adjacent to the selected cell | That cell becomes the offer, replacing any offer standing. |
| Any other cell, or no cell | Nothing changes. |

The release is what plays a move:

| At the release | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| An offer stands | The swap of the selected cell with the offered cell is requested, and the offer and the selection are both cleared. |
| No offer stands | The selection and the board stand as they are. |

So a player who takes hold of a gem, carries it onto a neighbor, and carries it
back where it came from has withdrawn the offer, and the release plays nothing.
A move is played only by a release with an offer standing.

Every requested swap goes through one acceptance path, and the move rules in
`specs/rules.md` decide it. An accepted swap runs the swap animation
`specs/rules.md` times before its first chain step resolves.

While an offer stands the two gems are drawn exchanged, as `specs/ui.md`
describes, so a player sees the move a release would play.

## Actions and bindings

`ACTIONS` holds the actions the game registers with the runtime, and `BINDINGS`
maps each action to the keys that fire it. Each action is read once per frame as
a press edge. The keyboard drives the menus; the board is played with the
pointer alone.

| Action | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `up` | Moves the menu highlight up. |
| `down` | Moves the menu highlight down. |
| `confirm` | Chooses the highlighted menu item. |
| `pause` | Enters and leaves `paused` from `playing`. |
| `mute` | Toggles the runtime's mute bit. |
| `back` | Leaves `howto` and `gameover`. |

Every key is a `KeyboardEvent.code`, so a binding is a physical key rather than
a layout-dependent character.

| Action | Keys |
| --- | --- |
| `up` | `ArrowUp` |
| `down` | `ArrowDown` |
| `confirm` | `Enter`, `Space` |
| `pause` | `Escape`, `KeyP` |
| `mute` | `KeyM` |
| `back` | `Escape` |

Each key listed for an action fires that action on its own, so either key of a
two-key action is enough. `Escape` fires both `pause` and `back`, and the two
act on screens that do not overlap: `pause` acts on `playing` and `paused` and
nowhere else, `back` on `howto` and `gameover` and nowhere else. So `Escape`
raises the pause menu from the board, drops it again, and backs out of every
other screen that can be backed out of.

The screens these actions move between, and where each one leads, are in
`specs/ui.md`.
